/-!
# The chart base change of a zero scheme, and the evaluation ideal along a morphism

Let `X` be an algebraic space over a scheme `S` contained in a big fppf site `B`, let `L` be an
invertible `𝒪_X`-module and let `s` be a global section of `L`.  The zero scheme `Z(s)` of
Stacks 0845 is a closed subspace of `X`, and over an étale scheme chart `V` of `X` it has two
presentations as a closed subscheme of the chart scheme:

* the chart base change `V ×_X Z(s)`, whose projection
  `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.closedSubspaceBaseChangeChartProj` to `V` is a closed immersion of
  schemes;
* the closed subscheme cut out by the quasi-coherent ideal sheaf which an *ideal of regular
  functions on the chart* induces, namely the evaluation ideal
  `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.sectionEvaluationIdealAt` of `s` at `V`.

**For an affine chart they are the same closed subscheme**, and the second is the ideal-sheaf
presentation of the zero scheme: on an affine scheme the scheme-level zero scheme of a section
of an invertible module is induced by exactly the ideal of global sections generated by the
values of the linear functionals at the section
(`Stacks.Divisors.zeroScheme_eq_ofIdealTop`), and that ideal is carried onto the evaluation
ideal by any linear equivalence matching the two readings of the sections over the chart
(`Ideal.span_range_dual_congr`).  So this is the compatibility of Stacks 0845 with étale chart
base change, read on ideal sheaves.

The scheme-level zero scheme of the chart restriction of `L` is *not* written out here.
`Stacks.Divisors.zeroScheme` takes the invertibility of its module as an instance argument, and
no declaration provides `AlgebraicGeometry.SheafOfModules.IsInvertible` for the restriction of an
invertible module to a chart scheme, so that term cannot be formed.  The two statements above are
what the identification reduces to once that instance and the sections dictionary
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.restrictToChartTopLinearEquiv` are supplied.

The second half of the file compares evaluation ideals across a morphism of algebraic spaces.
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.sectionEvaluationIdealAt_le_map` compares the evaluation ideals of one
section over two charts of *one* algebraic space;
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.sectionEvaluationIdealAt_modulePullback_le_map` compares the evaluation
ideal of a section `s` of `L` on a chart `V` of `X` with the evaluation ideal of its inverse image
`f^*s` on a chart `U` of `X'` over `V`, for a morphism `f : X' ⟶ X`.

## Main definitions

* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.closedSubspaceBaseChangeChartIsoZeroSchemeSubscheme`: the
  identification, over the chart, of the chart base change `V ×_X Z(s)` with the closed
  subscheme the evaluation ideal cuts out.

## Main results

* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.ker_closedSubspaceBaseChangeChartProj_zeroScheme`: **the kernel of
  the projection of `V ×_X Z(s)` is the ideal sheaf induced by the evaluation ideal of `s` at
  the chart**, for an affine chart and an invertible `L`.
* `cycleAssociatedToClosedSubscheme_closedSubspaceBaseChangeChartProj_zeroScheme`:
  the cycle of Stacks 02QU is the same on the two presentations, so a coefficient read on the
  chart base change may be read on the ideal-sheaf presentation instead, and
  `cycleCoeffOfClosedSubspace_zeroScheme_eq_cycleAssociatedToClosedSubscheme_chart`:
  **the left-hand side of Stacks 0EQL read on the ideal-sheaf presentation**.
* `Stacks.Divisors.zeroScheme_eq_ofIdealTop`: on an affine scheme the zero scheme of a section
  of an invertible module is the ideal sheaf induced by the ideal of global sections generated by
  the values of the functionals at the section, and `Ideal.span_range_dual_congr`: that ideal is
  invariant under a linear equivalence of the modules.  Together these say that the ideal sheaf
  appearing above *is* the scheme-level zero scheme of the chart restriction of `L`.
* `Ideal.mem_map_span_range_dual_of_free`: **dual base change for a module free of rank one**,
  with no flatness and no finite presentation hypothesis: a functional on `C ⊗[A] M` takes
  `1 ⊗ x` into the extension to `C` of the ideal of `A` generated by the values at `x` of the
  `A`-functionals on `M`.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.sectionEvaluationIdealAt_modulePullback_le_map`: **the evaluation
  ideal of an inverse image section is contained in the extension of the evaluation ideal of the
  section**, over a commutative diagram of étale scheme charts, when the module is free of rank
  one on the chart of the target.

## Implementation notes

`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.ker_closedSubspaceBaseChangeChartProj_zeroScheme` composes three facts:
the kernel of the projection of the chart base change *is* the chart ideal sheaf
(`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.ker_closedSubspaceBaseChangeChartProj`, by `rfl`), over an affine chart
that ideal sheaf is induced by the defining ideal of the closed subspace at the chart
(`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.ker_chartBaseChangeHom`), and the defining ideal of the zero scheme is
the evaluation ideal of the section (`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.definingIdealAt_zeroScheme_eq`, whose
input is discharged for an invertible module by
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.exists_definingIdealAt_eq_sectionEvaluationIdealAt_of_isInvertible`, that
is Stacks 0846). Affineness of the chart enters only in the second step, where an ideal sheaf on an
affine scheme is determined by its ideal of global sections.

The identification of the two closed subschemes is then the universal property of a closed
immersion with a prescribed kernel, `AlgebraicGeometry.IsClosedImmersion.lift` together with
`AlgebraicGeometry.IsClosedImmersion.isIso_lift`, exactly as in
`StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Spaces.Chow.ClosedSubspaceChartKerComparison`, and the
cycle-level consequence is
`Stacks.Chow.cycleAssociatedToClosedSubscheme_eq_of_iso`.

**The evaluation ideal comparison needs no flatness.**  The general dual base change
`Ideal.mem_map_span_range_dual` asks for `M` finitely presented over `A` and `C` flat over `A`,
and the second of those fails for the morphisms this comparison is wanted for — the inverse
image along a closed immersion of algebraic spaces, where `C` is a quotient of `A`.  For `M` free
of rank one both hypotheses are unnecessary: `x = l₀(x) • t`, so `1 ⊗ x` is the scalar
`l₀(x)` times `1 ⊗ t` and every functional sends it into the extension of the ideal generated by
`l₀(x)`.  That is `Ideal.mem_map_span_range_dual_of_free`, and it is what makes
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.sectionEvaluationIdealAt_modulePullback_le_map` hold over an arbitrary
diagram of charts, with neither chart assumed affine and with no quasi-coherence hypothesis: only
the *canonical* comparison
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.modulePullbackSectionsBaseChange` is used, and only its value at
`1 ⊗ s|_V`, which is the unit section
(`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.modulePullbackSectionsBaseChange_one_tmul`) and hence the value over
the chart of the inverse image of the global section
(`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.modulePullbackSections_val` read through
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.modulePullbackUnitSection_eq_modulePullbackChartSection`).  No
surjectivity or injectivity of that comparison is used, which is why the existence-only cartesian
statement `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.nonempty_modulePullbackSectionsBaseChange_linearEquiv` is not
needed either.

**Freeness of rank one is carried as chart data**, a generator `t` of the sections over the chart
together with a coordinate functional `l₀` satisfying `l₀ x • t = x`, exactly as
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.sectionEvaluationIdealAt_eq_span_singleton` and
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.definingIdealAt_zeroScheme_eq_span_generator_of_isInvertible` carry it.
For an invertible module such data exists on a cover of `X` by Stacks 0B8Q, in the form
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.exists_affine_chart_free_of_chart`.

Only the containment of the inverse-image evaluation ideal in the extension is proved.  The
reverse containment is the statement that the unit sections generate the sections of `f^*L` over
the chart, which is
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.modulePullbackSectionsBaseChange_surjective_iff_span_eq_top`; for a
quasi-coherent `L` and affine charts
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.nonempty_modulePullbackSectionsBaseChange_linearEquiv` provides an
isomorphism of the two sides but, as its implementation notes record, not bijectivity of the
canonical comparison, so it does not give the reverse containment.

